We can solve this system of equations by multiplying the first equation by 3 and the second equation by 4, and then subtracting the resulting equations to eliminate $q$:
\begin{align*}
3(3p+4q) &= 3(8) \\
4(4p+3q) &= 4(13) \\
\end{align*}
Simplifying, we get:
\begin{align*}
9p+12q &= 24 \\
16p+12q &= 52 \\
\end{align*}
Subtracting the second equation from the first, we have:
\begin{align*}
(9p+12q)-(16p+12q) &= 24-52 \\
-7p &= -28 \\
p &= 4 \\
\end{align*}
Substituting this value of $p$ into either of the original equations, we can solve for $q$:
\begin{align*}
4p+3q &= 13 \\
4(4)+3q &= 13 \\
16+3q &= 13 \\
3q &= -3 \\
q &= \boxed{-1} \\
\end{align*}
The answer is -1

